Z||000014||Formula88||06-04-2001||11:17 PM||||Well, it seems that "pop" is a Northern term and "soda" is a Southern term.  But in the deep south, they also call it "Pe-si"  Just drop the "p" from the pronuciation.  And no, that doesn't necessarily mean Pepsi - but any brown carbonated beverage, other than Root Beer.  We have reverence for that down here.  <P>We may be ignorant, but we've got our priorities.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> As soon as I figger out what they are, I'll let you alls know.  <P>My apologies to the rednecks in the audience.||||reg||1
Z||000015||kslish||06-04-2001||11:31 PM||||Here in Pittsburgh, it's definately pop.<P>Of course we have a language all our own...<P>Jumbo (bologna), gumbands (rubber bands), chipped-chopped ham (paper thin sliced ham), city chicken (breaded pork and veal cubes on a stick and grilled), worsh (wash) and on and on and on.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
